---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import StackDiagram from "../components/StackDiagram.astro";
import TakeATinCta from "../components/TakeATinCta.astro";
import { tins, tinsByTier, TIER_LABEL } from "../data/tins";

const description =
  "An exploration of running data workflows on Mojo: a native Apache Iceberg stack — Parquet, Avro, Thrift, codecs, Roaring bitmaps, object storage and threads — published as tins on mojoshelf and gated against the reference implementation of every format.";

const tinTiers = ["table", "format", "primitive", "oracle"] as const;
---

<Base title="Data lake building blocks in Mojo" description={description} bare={false}>
  <Hero />

  <!-- ============================ what this is ============================ -->
  <section class="section section--alt" id="what">
    <div class="wrap wrap--wide">
      <p class="eyebrow">What this is</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">An experiment, not a product</h2>

      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Now that Mojo has reached 1.0 I used Claude to see if one can implement
            a meaningfully large data library in Mojo. I set my sights on
            <a href="https://iceberg.apache.org" rel="noopener">Apache Iceberg</a>.
            The following formats are implemented rather than wrapped: Parquet, Avro, Thrift,
            Iceberg itself, Roaring bitmaps, Snappy and the hashes all decode bytes in
            Mojo instead of delegating. Some very mature C libraries are currently
            linked, not reimplemented — <strong>libzstd</strong>,
            <strong>liblz4</strong> and <strong>libbrotli</strong> for those three codecs,
            <strong>libcurl</strong> for HTTP
            and TLS, and <strong>pthreads</strong> for the threads the language does not yet
            provide. What is gone is the Python and JVM layer that every other route from
            Mojo to Iceberg has to go through.
          </p>
          <p>
            What came out is the stack below. Some of the answers are encouraging — native
            Parquet decode is <em>faster</em> than pyarrow on a single core. Some are not:
            writes are slower, thread-pooled scans lose, and the language is still missing
            pieces a data stack assumes. I don't think the misses are limitations of the
            approach or the tech stack, it is a matter of putting in more time and tokens.
          </p>
        </div>

        <aside class="callout">
          <h3 class="callout__title">Packaged on mojoshelf</h3>
          <p>
            Magmalake leans on the packaging infrastructure defined by
            <a href="https://mojoshelf.org" rel="noopener">mojoshelf.org</a> — a registry for
            Mojo <em>tins</em> on top of pixi. Every tin here is published there and installable with one
            command.
          </p>
          <pre class="callout__code"><code>pixi shelf add iceberg-mojo</code></pre>
          <p class="callout__fine">
            Working with a coding agent? <code>npx skills add mojoshelf/mojoshelf --skill
            mojoshelf-consume --yes</code> teaches it to find and install tins on its own —
            it installs the <code>shelf</code> CLI too.
          </p>
          <p class="callout__fine">
            Tins are <strong>git source dependencies, not conda-channel packages</strong> —
            <code>pixi add</code> will not find them, which is the first thing people trip on.
            <code>pixi shelf add</code> resolves the tin and everything it depends on.
          </p>
          <p class="callout__fine">
            Source tins can be consumed as git submodules, or as pixi git source dependencies.
            The pixi approach is recommended because it encapsulates any additional build process.
          </p>
          <a class="callout__link" href="https://mojoshelf.org" rel="noopener">
            mojoshelf.org
            <svg width="11" height="11" viewBox="0 0 12 12" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"
                 stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
              <path d="M3 9 9 3M4.2 3H9v4.8" />
            </svg>
          </a>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================== the stack ============================= -->
  <section class="section" id="stack">
    <div class="wrap wrap--wide">
      <p class="eyebrow">The stack</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">How they layer</h2>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
        Iceberg sits on Parquet, Avro, Roaring and objectstore; those sit on Thrift, the
        compression codecs and the hashes; threads runs alongside all of it. I kept them
        separate, so you can take just the one you need.
      </p>
      <StackDiagram />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================ tins at a glance ========================= -->
  <section class="section section--alt" id="tins">
    <div class="wrap wrap--wide">
      <p class="eyebrow">The tins</p>
      <h2 class="section-title">Tins at a glance</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        {tins.filter((t) => t.tier !== "oracle").length} tins, each independently usable and
        checked against the reference implementation of the format it speaks. What each one is
        and what proves it lives on <a href="/tins">the tins page</a>.
      </p>

      <div class="tin-glance">
        {
          tinTiers.map((tier) => (
            <div class="tier">
              <h3 class="tier__label">{TIER_LABEL[tier]}</h3>
              <ul class="tin-glance__list">
                {tinsByTier(tier).map((t) => (
                  <li>
                    <a href="/tins">
                      <span class="tin-glance__name">{t.name}</span>
                      <span class="tin-glance__version">{t.version}</span>
                    </a>
                  </li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================= take a tin ============================= -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap wrap--wide">
      <TakeATinCta />
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .tin-glance__list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.6rem;
  }
  .tin-glance__list a {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    padding: 0.45rem 0.8rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    background: var(--surface);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: border-color 0.15s ease, box-shadow 0.15s ease;
  }
  .tin-glance__list a:hover {
    border-color: var(--border-strong);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow);
  }
  .tin-glance__name {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text);
  }
  .tin-glance__version {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    color: var(--text-faint);
  }
</style>
